WEBVTT

00:02.040 --> 00:07.560
Now with all these bindings you hopefully got an overview of how you can work with

00:07.560 --> 00:08.060
Svelte

00:08.060 --> 00:12.720
and its bindings to conveniently get access to foreign values and using two-way binding here

00:12.720 --> 00:16.900
really is extremely helpful and you'll often use it when working with forms.

00:17.880 --> 00:22.900
There is another way of getting access to any element on the page though, so besides

00:22.900 --> 00:23.400
using

00:23.400 --> 00:26.740
two-way binding and also not limited to inputs.

00:27.320 --> 00:31.400
let me first demonstrate it with the help of an input though let's say here we

00:31.400 --> 00:32.920
have an input and

00:32.920 --> 00:37.420
we have a button and i only need to value once i clicked that button so

00:37.420 --> 00:41.640
here in on click i'll

00:41.640 --> 00:47.060
trigger save data which is a function i have yet to add so let's do that

00:47.060 --> 00:48.880
quickly here we have

00:50.340 --> 00:55.360
save data and i'm not accepting any event object in here because that would be my

00:55.360 --> 00:59.200
click event and I'm not interested in that but I do want to get the text

00:59.200 --> 01:00.300
that was entered into

01:00.300 --> 01:05.780
this input. Of course we could set up two-way binding with bind value and then

01:05.780 --> 01:06.800
storing that

01:06.800 --> 01:11.620
data which is in that input in some variable. But if I only need this in

01:11.620 --> 01:13.240
a read-only way and I never

01:13.240 --> 01:16.940
set the value anywhere else I could do it and it would not be wrong and

01:16.940 --> 01:18.000
in most cases you will

01:18.000 --> 01:23.300
probably do it with two-way binding but you can actually also simply read the value

01:23.300 --> 01:23.980
from the input.

01:23.980 --> 01:30.700
So of course, what we could do here is we could console.log, document, query, selector,

01:31.340 --> 01:36.360
and then maybe give this some ID, username.

01:36.960 --> 01:39.480
So hashtag username selects by ID.

01:39.800 --> 01:43.920
Let's now give this here the ID, username, so that this is selected.

01:44.280 --> 01:46.360
And now we get access to this element.

01:46.360 --> 01:50.860
And since it's input element, this will have a value property.

01:51.380 --> 01:53.540
And if we do that and we save that all,

01:53.740 --> 01:57.440
and I enter super or whatever you want here,

01:57.600 --> 01:59.320
and I click save, you see that being printed.

01:59.760 --> 02:02.680
This is not the svelte-ish way of doing it though.

02:02.980 --> 02:06.520
This is vanilla JavaScript code, which you can use,

02:06.680 --> 02:08.340
but there's a more elegant solution.

02:08.560 --> 02:11.400
And you might remember that I used a similar approach

02:11.400 --> 02:13.960
earlier in the course where I also used query selector

02:13.960 --> 02:16.640
because I had no better tool for the job.

02:17.360 --> 02:19.120
Now, actually, there is a better tool.

02:19.120 --> 02:22.580
You can use binding, but a special form of binding.

02:23.240 --> 02:26.000
So instead of relying on selecting this by ID,

02:27.220 --> 02:28.860
you can instead add bind,

02:28.980 --> 02:30.580
but now you don't bind to value

02:30.580 --> 02:32.220
because I don't want two-way binding,

02:32.540 --> 02:33.480
but to this,

02:33.680 --> 02:37.060
which is simply a special syntax understood by Svelte.

02:37.240 --> 02:40.180
This thing here is not a normal attribute

02:40.180 --> 02:41.560
you could add to an input.

02:41.740 --> 02:43.900
This is simply understood by the Svelte compiler.

02:44.160 --> 02:45.800
And by binding to this,

02:45.800 --> 02:49.800
you can store a reference to that element here,

02:49.960 --> 02:53.040
a pointer to that element in a variable of your choice.

02:53.440 --> 02:55.600
So now we can add a new variable,

02:56.300 --> 02:57.840
username input, for example.

02:58.460 --> 03:01.440
And this is the variable which I'll use down there,

03:01.700 --> 03:02.720
username input.

03:03.040 --> 03:06.260
So now a pointer at this HTML element

03:06.260 --> 03:08.860
or at this DOM element, to be precise,

03:09.180 --> 03:11.400
is stored in username input.

03:11.800 --> 03:14.160
And therefore here in save data,

03:14.160 --> 03:18.800
I can now console.log username input.value

03:18.800 --> 03:21.340
because this points at this HTML element.

03:21.780 --> 03:25.020
And hence now if I enter super here and I click save,

03:25.660 --> 03:27.060
you see that still works,

03:27.220 --> 03:28.780
but of course that's way shorter

03:28.780 --> 03:31.240
than manually selecting this with query selector.

03:31.480 --> 03:33.920
And we also don't have to add the ID.

03:34.120 --> 03:34.940
We can remove that

03:34.940 --> 03:37.520
because now this reference and connection

03:37.520 --> 03:39.700
is set up behind the scenes by Svelte.

03:39.980 --> 03:41.540
Now this is not two-way binding.

03:41.740 --> 03:43.980
This is really just a pointer at the full element.

03:44.160 --> 03:46.380
We're not restricted to reading its value.

03:46.580 --> 03:47.740
We can do whatever we want.

03:47.880 --> 03:50.900
We can manipulate it, though I would not recommend that.

03:51.100 --> 03:53.140
You shouldn't change it programmatically.

03:53.140 --> 03:56.460
You should always change your DOM through Svelte

03:56.460 --> 03:58.220
by the instructions you place down there.

03:58.420 --> 04:00.640
But you can read any data you want.

04:00.820 --> 04:03.240
You can check out the CSS classes on the element.

04:03.400 --> 04:06.040
You can do whatever you want because if we log it,

04:06.140 --> 04:07.900
you'll see it's just a full element.

04:08.180 --> 04:12.340
So if a console log this here, and I press save again,

04:12.500 --> 04:13.560
it's the full input.

04:14.160 --> 04:18.080
way if you want to inspect it here you can use console dir and now you

04:18.080 --> 04:20.420
get a inspectable javascript

04:20.420 --> 04:26.060
object so this is really the full dom element and this does not just work with

04:26.060 --> 04:28.240
html input elements

04:28.240 --> 04:33.520
it works with any html element and your own components as well so we can also

04:33.520 --> 04:34.460
use a div

04:34.460 --> 04:41.980
instead and bind this to some div which of course is a variable i have yet

04:41.980 --> 04:43.140
to add so here is

04:43.780 --> 04:49.780
some div and now for simplicity when we click save data i can now also console

04:49.780 --> 04:51.200
there there

04:52.840 --> 04:58.440
some div so it's the same button as before but now i'm binding this here this

04:58.440 --> 04:59.600
div to the sum div

05:00.340 --> 05:05.300
variable and therefore i can now interact with the div and inspect it and do whatever

05:05.300 --> 05:05.680
i want

05:05.680 --> 05:09.980
again changing something there is not recommended you should always do that with the help of

05:09.980 --> 05:10.460
swells

05:10.460 --> 05:14.520
normal syntax, but reading values from it can be really useful.

